{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the idle-alarm repair at head a11896d131f33e5240ce062c1a8f82a3b31b76db, tree 15a3d127, for request c3034274 and review request b2f6ead8. Verdict: APPROVED.\n\nThe head adds nine commits to main 901a7fa4 and changes eight paths: `outbox.ts`, `site/token-reuse.ts` and `worker.ts` in `packages/scope/src`, the two owning tests, the seal test, and the implementation note with its manifest. I read the implementation note first, then the source diff in context, the tests and the sealed evidence.\n\n## What the head fixes\n\nThe request asked whether dap leaves alarms armed on quiescent scopes. The head finds one such case and fixes it.\n\nWhen a Site read fails before `createToken`, for example on a wrong remote, the cleanup deletes the reservation but never reschedules. The ScopeIndex scheduler also never cleared its alarm when no Site row remained. The result was an orphan alarm on an empty ScopeIndex. The repair has two parts:\n- `token-reuse.ts:167-174`: after the pre-mint cleanup deletes its own reservation, it calls the scheduler within the flight deadline. A `catch` keeps the original answer.\n- `worker.ts:149-151`: when `wake()` is null and an alarm exists, the loop deletes the alarm. A live wake keeps the old rule: set an absent or later alarm, and keep an earlier one.\n\n## Against request c3034274\n\n1. The alarm-arming sites. Resolved. The note gives each owner of the one ScopeObject alarm and says when each one clears: timed work, outbox dispatch, outside attempts, the driver continuation, signer maintenance, archive and credential expiry. It names the ScopeIndex Site alarm as the only other persisted owner. I confirmed that `object.ts:200` and `worker.ts:146-151` hold every `setAlarm`, `deleteAlarm` and `getAlarm` write in `packages/scope/src`. The note also says a null alarm does not prove that work has settled, because a parked attempt can have `next` null. That limit is right.\n2. The quiet-room witness. Resolved. `idle-alarms.test.ts` builds a host-none room and finds all six journals by following the create sends. It then checks that every journal has an empty outbox, no timers, no deadline and a null `getAlarm()`, across reads and across eviction and reactivation. The room's own index has no Site rows and no alarm.\n3. The one-timed-rule witness. Resolved. One invitation arms only Membership, at its exact deadline, also after eviction. Running the stored alarm appends the timed input, moves the head by one and lapses the invitation. All six alarms are then null.\n4. The fix in the same head. Resolved. The two new Site cases cover both sides of the delete. With no other row, a pre-mint failure leaves no row and a null alarm. With a ready row, the same failure keeps its generation, its row and its expiry alarm. Each P1 control removes one half of the repair, and the unchanged named assertion then fails with an ordinary assertion at line 225.\n\nThe repair removes an orphan alarm on the one ScopeIndex object. It cannot account for about 700 cells that restore with no request. The note says so, and parent finding a6847815 keeps the celld attribution open.\n\n## The gates\n\n- Gate 1 at P2 failed on the source seal of `ScopeIndex`, and it stays failed.\n- F1 and F2 changed only seal lines in `scripts/security-entry-points.test.mjs`. The native run after F2 stopped at its 32 MiB output limit and stays inconclusive.\n- P3 gave the room witness its own ScopeIndex through the `StartupOwner` namespace seam. That change reaches outside the single-file exception, so ruling 31962e1f needs one further full gate.\n- Gate 2 at P3, b69d58cb with tree c5b5eea6, passes all five stages. Its receipt has SHA-256 fa83d34f, exit 0, and the same head and tree before and after.\n- P3 to the head changes only the note and the manifest, which the documents-only rule in `docs/testing.md` covers.\n\n## Checks at this head\n\n- The worktree is clean. `git merge-tree --write-tree` onto main 909d8a1a gives a clean tree, 22a9e44b.\n- I reran the owning files once each, with no whole suite:\n  - `security-entry-points.test.mjs`: 10 of 10;\n  - `site-token-reuse.test.ts` and `idle-alarms.test.ts`: 8 of 8.\n- The builder worktree stayed clean after the runs.\n- The manifest has SHA-256 46c7fa40 and 28,861 bytes. It follows ruling 4e199bd8:\n  - all 193 listed files match by size and SHA-256;\n  - `finalization` and `operator-inputs` match their digests, and each SHA256SUMS reruns clean, with no unlisted file;\n  - the root SHA256SUMS, digest 9fa4346c, covers all 405 other files, with none unlisted and none writable.\n- check-doc-links on an export of the head: 760 links, 0 broken.\n- check-notes from 901a7fa4: 0 warnings.\n- check-commit-messages from 901a7fa4: 9 checked, 0 flagged.\n- `git diff --check`: clean.\n\n## Points that do not block\n\n1. `#scheduleSiteTokens` in `worker.ts:139-155` keeps a narrow window, and the window predates this head. The loop can finish its last revision check before the `finally` clears `#siteAlarm`. A caller that runs in that gap increments the revision and gets back the settled promise, so the scheduler never processes that increment. A new reservation could then go without its alarm until the next acquisition or alarm. Clearing `#siteAlarm` inside the async body, after the loop, closes the gap. The repair does not widen it.\n2. The note is hard to read. It uses slashes (\"zero rows/mints\", \"generation/key/state-scoped\", \"failure/unknown\"), and many of its sentences carry three or four claims. The technical-writing rule applies to implementation notes too.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe repair stays inside the ScopeIndex Site scheduler and the pre-mint cleanup. ScopeIndex has no other alarm owner, so the new delete cannot clear another owner's wake. The ScopeObject alarm, the outbox algorithm and every turn path are unchanged. The `outbox.ts` change is a comment, and it now lists all seven causes. Approved.\n\n## Security\n\n- Generation custody holds. The cleanup still deletes only its own reserved generation, and the scheduler keeps a successor's alarm or an earlier one.\n- The change does not touch mint-entered ambiguity, the `unavailable` path, token lifetimes, plaintext erasure or provider revocation.\n- A scheduling failure in the cleanup keeps the original answer. `Promise.race` handles a late rejection.\n- The seal changes record the two reviewed production deltas, and the route and authority gates are unchanged.\n\nApproved.\n\n## Simplification\n\nThe production repair is 11 added and 2 removed lines, and it reuses the existing flight deadline and revision loop. The room witness reuses `StartupOwner` and the native fixture lifetime with explicit bounds. Approved.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved.\n- Security: approved.\n- Simplification: approved.\n- The request's checks: the alarm inventory, both witnesses and the Site fix hold in the source, the tests, the controls and gate 2. The evidence verifies under ruling 4e199bd8. Gate 1 stays failed, and the first native run stays inconclusive.\n\nThe landing check will follow the landing.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:46eb90acb13433e13d9f4eb6ea7ba08ae8096577","binding":"assigned","head":"a11896d131f33e5240ce062c1a8f82a3b31b76db","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:42c846e5d743aa7daea7da8f2472c00a458489e8\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d19e54721d55375a9c6b1f9476acbb44eee3760b\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:42c846e5d743aa7daea7da8f2472c00a458489e8","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}